# Read one of your own S3 buckets.
# - S3's virtual-hosted URL names the bucket in the host, <bucket>.s3.<region>.amazonaws.com, so
#   the exact host already allows that bucket and no other. The tree then keeps the reach to one
#   key prefix within the bucket.
# - The proxy refuses and logs a key outside the prefix, or one spelled outside a rule path's
#   canonical form (doc/egress-proxy.md, "The rule file").
# - A bucket whose name contains dots does not fit S3's wildcard certificate and takes the
#   path-style form instead. There the region's host serves every bucket, and the bucket is the
#   first segment: allow https://s3.<region>.amazonaws.com/<bucket>/ read, the line gcs-bucket
#   narrows.
# - The line grants reads only: a presigned upload URL still fails here, since the proxy refuses a
#   PUT where no line grants it.
# - What travels in an allowed GET's suffix and query is not bounded (SECURITY.md, "Exfiltration
#   through allowed network traffic").
allow https://my-bucket.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/public/ read
